moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Financing costs rising to 12.3% of net revenue stream in 2027/28, from 8.0% in 2026/27","lead":"Rising financing costs reduce the council's financial flexibility and indicate an increasing debt servicing burden relative to revenue available for service delivery, which constrains borrowing capacity and service spending headroom","iso":"2026-09-09","paper_title":"2026-2027 Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Supplementary","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39481/39481.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Financing costs are projected to rise from \u00a32.9m to \u00a311.8m over three years, doubling as a proportion of revenue","lead":"A 2.6x increase in projected financing costs suggests substantial new borrowing or capital expenditure planned, which affects budget flexibility, liquidity risk, and repayment capacity. Lenders would need to understand what capital schemes are driving this\u2026","iso":"2026-09-09","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026 Supplementary","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39480/39480.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Commercial property asset Ashdown House generating zero return on \u00a34.5m valuation","lead":"Non-performing commercial assets consume balance sheet space and capital without contributing to the council's financial position. Lenders assess whether commercial property portfolios are actively managed and generating returns. A zero-return property of\u2026","iso":"2026-09-09","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39326/39326.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-2027 Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Supplementary","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39481/39481.pdf","headline":"Financing costs rising to 12.3% of net revenue stream in 2027/28, from 8.0% in 2026/27","detail":"The corrected forecast shows financing costs as a proportion of net revenue stream rising sharply from 8.0% in 2026/27 to 12.3% in 2027/28, with a slight fall to 11.2% in 2028/29. In absolute terms, financing costs increase from \u00a36,681k to \u00a310,512k over that year.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rising financing costs reduce the council's financial flexibility and indicate an increasing debt servicing burden relative to revenue available for service delivery, which constrains borrowing capacity and service spending headroom","figures":"Financing costs: \u00a36,681k (2026/27), \u00a310,512k (2027/28), \u00a39,806k (2028/29); Proportion of net revenue stream: 8.0%, 12.3%, 11.2%","quote":"Proportion of net revenue stream   8.0% 12.3% 11.2%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026 Supplementary","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39480/39480.pdf","headline":"Financing costs are projected to rise from \u00a32.9m to \u00a311.8m over three years, doubling as a proportion of revenue","detail":"The corrected treasury outturn table shows financing costs (capital borrowing and long-term obligations net of investment income) rising from \u00a32,910k in 2025/26 to \u00a37,661k in 2026/27 and \u00a311,830k in 2027/28. Expressed as a proportion of net revenue stream, these costs will climb from 3.5% to 8.9% to 13.7% respectively, a dramatic acceleration in the council's capital and financing burden.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A 2.6x increase in projected financing costs suggests substantial new borrowing or capital expenditure planned, which affects budget flexibility, liquidity risk, and repayment capacity. Lenders would need to understand what capital schemes are driving this trajectory.","figures":"\u00a32,910k (2025/26), \u00a37,661k (2026/27), \u00a311,830k (2027/28); as proportions: 3.5%, 8.9%, 13.7%","quote":"Financing  as a proportion of net revenue stream    2.7% 3.5% 8.9% 13.7%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39326/39326.pdf","headline":"Commercial property asset Ashdown House generating zero return on \u00a34.5m valuation","detail":"Ashdown House, a commercial investment property valued at \u00a34.5m, generated zero income in 2025/26. The asset is generating no return and appears to be either vacant, underoccupied, or held for non-financial reasons, representing dead capital at a time when the council needs to maximize investment yield.","category":"income","matters":"Non-performing commercial assets consume balance sheet space and capital without contributing to the council's financial position. Lenders assess whether commercial property portfolios are actively managed and generating returns. A zero-return property of this size raises questions about portfolio stewardship.","figures":"Purchase cost \u00a37,915k; Fair value \u00a34,464k; Income \u00a30; Rate of return 0%","quote":"Ashdown House 7,915 4,464 - -","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39326/39326.pdf","headline":"Net borrowing forecast to rise 78% from \u00a3134m to \u00a3239m by 2027/28","detail":"Current net borrowing stands at \u00a3134.2m. The council's medium-term forecast shows net borrowing rising to \u00a3200.6m in 2026/27 and \u00a3239.2m in 2027/28. This substantial increase in debt, combined with rising PWLB rates (10-year rate rose from 5.38% to 5.72%), compounds the financing cost pressure documented above.","category":"borrowing","matters":"An 78% increase in net borrowing over two years signals the council is becoming increasingly leveraged. Combined with financing costs rising to 47.7% of revenue, this trajectory is unsustainable without either significant revenue growth, asset sales, or service reduction.","figures":"2025/26 actual: \u00a3134,245k; 2026/27 budget: \u00a3200,647k; 2027/28 budget: \u00a3239,201k","quote":"Net borrowing 146,887 134,245 200,647 239,201","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-09","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 2025 \u2013 2026","paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39326/39326.pdf","headline":"General Fund capital program delivered at only 48% of budget; HRA at 60%","detail":"General Fund capital expenditure budgeted at \u00a342.3m but only \u00a320.3m spent; HRA capital budgeted at \u00a347.2m but only \u00a328.5m spent. These substantial underspends suggest either delivery constraints, capacity limitations, or inability to progress planned investment despite earlier budget approval.","category":"capital","matters":"Repeated capital underspends indicate the council cannot execute its own capital program, which raises questions about project management, governance, and underlying financial capacity. For lenders, this suggests the council's capital plans may not be reliable.","figures":"General Fund: Budget \u00a342,267k, Actual \u00a320,282k (48%); HRA: Budget \u00a347,188k, Actual \u00a328,479k (60%)","quote":"Capital expenditure 11,673 42,267 20,282 ... Capital expenditure 21,819 47,188 28,479","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-09"},"name":"Crawley","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"Financing costs rising to 12.3% of net revenue stream in 2027/28, from 8.0% in 2026/27","lead":"Rising financing costs reduce the council's financial flexibility and indicate an increasing debt servicing burden relative to revenue available for service delivery, which constrains borrowing capacity and service spending headroom","also":["7 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Overview and Scrutiny Commission on 2026-10-05","n_findings":8,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"2026-2027 Budget Monitoring - Quarter 1 Supplementary","top_paper_url":"https://crawley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s39481/39481.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":52,"news_drafts_total":3}}